**Concrete arguments and replacements**

**PROVED — the common evaluation assertion is false.** Take
\[
N=119,\quad m=11,\quad \alpha_1=\sqrt2,\quad \alpha_2=-\sqrt2.
\]
Each separate order admits \(\alpha_j\mapsto11\), since \(11^2\equiv2\pmod{119}\). But their compositum satisfies \(\alpha_1+\alpha_2=0\), whereas the proposed images sum to \(22\ne0\). A map from the tensor-product presentation need not descend through its additional relations.

This does not invalidate abstract TI or the per-field certificate used by NR-q.

**KNOWN / PROVED — KT’s algebraic core survives.** Fact (F) follows from Dixon–Mortimer, *Permutation Groups*, Theorem 5.2B, p. 150, with \(r=2\); the exceptional and imprimitive indices exceed \(d+1\) for \(d\ge7\). [Primary reference](https://ebooks.karbust.me/Mathematics/John%20D.%20Dixon%2C%20Brian%20Mortimer%20-%20Permutation%20Groups%20%281996%29%20%5B978-1-4612-0731-3%5D.pdf).

For even root-set size \(n\ge6\), a kernel permutation on
\(\mathbf F_2[X]^0/\langle\mathbf1\rangle\) must fix every two-element subset modulo complementation. A two-element subset cannot be another’s complement when \(n\ge6\); hence it fixes every pair and is the identity. The stated hyperelliptic module description is correct. [Morgan, §8.1](https://londmathsoc.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/full/10.1112/plms.70066).

The subsequent generator-height claim fails:
\[
H_{f(x-1)}\le(d+1)2^dH_f,\qquad
\psi(\alpha+1)=\frac{g_0+g_1}{g_1}.
\]
Thus another generator and another small evaluated fraction need not have Arakelov-sized height. These coordinate changes do not prove fresh randomness, but they refute the stated inference.

**MODEL — repaired R3.** Put \(M=\Lambda U\). For algebraic targets define
\[
C_v=X_v-z_v;
\]
for rational targets set \(C_v=X_v\). The correct recurrence is
\[
C_v\le M+\sum_w z_w,\qquad
z_w\le\frac{2C_w}{d-2}.
\]
For a tree with at most \(\kappa(d-2)\) nodes and \(d\ge4\),
\[
C_v\le(1+2\kappa)M,\qquad
X_v\le2(1+2\kappa)M.
\]
Degree three has a separate bounded geometric sum. These constant changes suffice for R2’s window argument.

The original constant actually fails: with \(d=5,\kappa=1,M=1\), a three-node algebraic chain with negligible coefficient height has successive full norm logarithms
\[
\frac53,\quad\frac{25}{9},\quad\frac{95}{27}.
\]
The last exceeds \(1+2\kappa=3\).

**MODEL / PROVED calculation — chain floors.** For fixed \(r\ge1\), feasibility requires \(C\ge r\). Indeed, monotonic targets give
\[
v_j-\zeta_{j+1}\ge\delta,
\]
so the budget is at least \(r\delta\).

For \(C\ge r\), the exact fixed-depth optimum is
\[
\alpha_r(C)=
\begin{cases}
\displaystyle\max\!\left(
\frac{r+2}{3r+5},
\frac{r+2-C}{2r+5-2C}\right),&C<r+1,\\[4pt]
\displaystyle\frac{r+2}{3r+5},&C\ge r+1.
\end{cases}
\]
The saturation threshold is actually \(C=r+1-1/(r+1)\).

| \(C\) | \(\alpha\) | \(\delta\) | Targets | Norm exponents |
|---|---:|---:|---|---|
| \(1\) | \(2/5\) | \(1/5\) | \(3/5\) | \(4/5\) |
| \(3/2\) | \(3/8\) | \(1/4\) | \(5/8,1/2\) | \(7/8,3/4\) |
| \(3\) | \(4/11\) | \(3/11\) | \(7/11,6/11,5/11\) | \(10/11,9/11,8/11\) |

I checked these and additional transition points against the exact LP. The code’s `closed_budget(2, 1)` returns \(3/7\), but the LP rejects it. This error does not change the reported global minimum.

**PROVED — NR-q window repair.** With its stated \(Y=N^{1/(2k)}\), all \(k\)-products exceed \(\sqrt N\), so S″ cannot apply modulo \(N\) as claimed.

After preprocessing \(3\nmid N\), instead split modulo
\[
N_* =9^{k+1}N.
\]
The products lie in \((\sqrt N,2^k\sqrt N]\). One of its \(k\) dyadic windows contains the required fraction, and every upper endpoint \(X\) satisfies
\[
2X^2\le2\cdot4^kN<N_*.
\]
S″ now applies. The additional probability loss is \(\exp(-O(k))\), which is harmless because \(k=o(\Lambda_\alpha)\). Recursive halves remain \(N^{1/2+o(1)}\). CRT preserves the hidden-root uniformity modulo \(N\).

Thus the split defect is repairable without strengthening \(T_q\).

**PROVED — precisely where precision can still be amplified.** For any nonnegative integer certificate count,
\[
\Pr_A(Z\ge1)\ge\frac{(\mathbb E_AZ)^2}{\mathbb E_AZ^2};
\]
correlation causes no problem.

But let a target subset have density \(\varepsilon=e^{-c\Lambda}\), and put \(Z_{A,q}=1\) exactly on that subset, independently of \(A\). Then the relaxed **joint** moments satisfy
\[
\mathbb EZ=\varepsilon,\qquad
\frac{\mathbb EZ^2}{(\mathbb EZ)^2}=\varepsilon^{-1}.
\]
Every pool still covers only that subset. The split pays \(\varepsilon^{2k}\). Targetwise field moments on a polylogarithmic-density target set avoid this; jointly averaged moments alone do not.

Finally, tied fields can retain conditional randomness: fixing \(a\) and choosing fresh \(b\) gives
\[
\mathbf Q(\sqrt a),\quad\mathbf Q(\sqrt b),\quad\mathbf Q(\sqrt{ab}).
\]
Their dependence does not remove the free parameter \(b\). Useful evaluated transfer remains unproved, but membership in one Galois field does not exclude it. This is one concrete reason the Verdict’s exhaustive reduction is unjustified.